**user**:
> In Julia, I have a variable defined in one script, which I do include("one.jl"), and a function that uses that variable in another script (and I do include("two.jl")). Then I call the function in the REPL, and it complains that it can't find the first variable. Why?
**assistant**:
> This happens because of **scope and when code is evaluated**, not because `include` "forgets" variables.
> 
> In Julia, `include("file.jl")` is basically like pasting the contents of that file *at the point where `include` is called*, into the current module.
> 
> The key gotcha:  
> 
> ### 1. Functions capture *global bindings*, not names
> 
> Suppose you do this:
> 
> ```julia
> # one.jl
> x = 10
> ```
> 
> ```julia
> # two.jl
> f() = x + 1
> ```
> 
> Now at the REPL:
> 
> ```julia
> julia> include("two.jl")  # defines f()
> julia> include("one.jl")  # defines x
> julia> f()
> ERROR: UndefVarError: x not defined
> ```
> 
> Why? Because when `two.jl` was included, Julia defined `f` in the current module. Inside `f`, `x` is resolved as a global variable *in that module*. At the time `f` is created, there is no global `x` yet, but that’s actually fine — Julia doesn’t require it to exist at definition time.
> 
> The *real* problem here usually comes from a different pattern:
> 
> ### 2. Where are you including from?
> 
> If you do this:
> 
> ```julia
> julia> function load_stuff()
>            include("one.jl")  # defines x, but inside the *scope* of this function body
>            include("two.jl")  # defines f, but also inside this function body
>        end
> ```
> 
> Then:
> 
> - `x` and `f` are *local to the function body* `load_stuff`; they are not globals in `Main`.
> - Outside `load_stuff()`, at the REPL, `f()` will not see `x` as a global in `Main` and you get `UndefVarError: x not defined`.
> 
> So a common failure mode is:
> 
> - `include("one.jl")` is executed **inside a function or local scope**,
> - `include("two.jl")` is executed later (maybe at top level),
> - the function in `two.jl` tries to use `x` as a global, but that `x` only exists in the earlier local scope, not as a global.
> 
> ### 3. The safe pattern
> 
> If you want both scripts to share globals in the REPL or a module, you need to include them **at the same top-level scope** and in a sensible order:
> 
> ```julia
> julia> include("one.jl")  # defines global x in Main
> julia> include("two.jl")  # defines f() that expects global x in Main
> julia> f()
> 11
> ```
> 
> Or, better yet, put them in a module:
> 
> ```julia
> module MyStuff
>     include("one.jl")   # defines x in MyStuff
>     include("two.jl")   # defines f in MyStuff, which uses x
> end
> 
> julia> MyStuff.f()
> ```
> 
> ### 4. Why your REPL call fails
> 
> So the most likely reasons your function “can’t find” the variable are:
> 
> 1. `include("one.jl")` ran in a local scope (inside a function, let-block, etc.), so the variable is not a global where the function is defined/called.
> 2. You’re calling the function from a different module/namespace than where the variable lives.
> 3. Less common: the variable name is defined as a local in the function (e.g., via an argument or `local`), shadowing the global you expected.
> 
> If you paste your minimal example (two tiny files and the REPL commands you run), I can pinpoint exactly which of these is biting you and show the minimal fix.
**user**:
> Actually, I realized that two.jl defines a module, then defines the function inside that module, then exports that function. Is that the source of the problem?
**assistant**:
> Yes, that’s almost certainly the source of the problem.
> 
> What’s happening is:
> 
> - `one.jl` defines a variable `x` in `Main` (i.e., the REPL’s default module).
> - `two.jl` defines a **module** (say `ModTwo`) and inside it defines `f` that uses `x`.
> - Inside `ModTwo`, when Julia looks up `x`, it looks in `ModTwo` (and its imports), **not** in `Main`.
> - So `ModTwo.f()` doesn’t see `Main.x` and you get `UndefVarError: x not defined`.
> 
> A minimal sketch of your situation:
> 
> ```julia
> # one.jl
> x = 10
> ```
> 
> ```julia
> # two.jl
> module ModTwo
> export f
> 
> f() = x + 1   # <- x is looked up in ModTwo
> 
> end
> ```
> 
> At the REPL:
> 
> ```julia
> julia> include("one.jl")   # x in Main
> julia> include("two.jl")   # defines module ModTwo
> julia> using .ModTwo       # bring f into Main
> 
> julia> f()
> ERROR: UndefVarError: x not defined
> ```
> 
> Because from the perspective of `ModTwo`:
> 
> - There is no `x` defined inside `ModTwo`.
> - `Main.x` is not automatically visible inside `ModTwo`.
> 
> ---
> 
> ### Ways to fix it
> 
> Pick one of these, depending on what design you actually want.
> 
> #### Option 1: Put `x` inside the same module
> 
> If `x` is conceptually part of that module’s state, define it there:
> 
> ```julia
> # two.jl
> module ModTwo
> export f, x
> 
> x = 10
> f() = x + 1
> 
> end
> ```
> 
> Then:
> 
> ```julia
> julia> include("two.jl")
> julia> using .ModTwo
> julia> f()      # 11
> ```
> 
> If you really want `x` defined in `one.jl`, include that file *inside* the module:
> 
> ```julia
> # two.jl
> module ModTwo
> export f, x
> 
> include("one.jl")   # defines x in ModTwo
> f() = x + 1
> 
> end
> ```
> 
> Now `x` and `f` live in the same namespace (`ModTwo`).
> 
> #### Option 2: Explicitly refer to `Main.x`
> 
> If you truly mean “use the global defined in Main”:
> 
> ```julia
> module ModTwo
> export f
> 
> f() = Main.x + 1
> 
> end
> ```
> 
> Then:
> 
> ```julia
> julia> include("one.jl")   # defines Main.x
> julia> include("two.jl")   # defines ModTwo
> julia> using .ModTwo
> julia> f()                 # works, uses Main.x
> ```
> 
> This is explicit but couples the module directly to `Main`, which is often not what you want for reusable code.
> 
> #### Option 3: Pass the value in as an argument
> 
> More idiomatic, less global:
> 
> ```julia
> module ModTwo
> export f
> 
> f(x) = x + 1
> 
> end
> ```
> 
> Then:
> 
> ```julia
> julia> include("one.jl")   # x in Main
> julia> include("two.jl")
> julia> using .ModTwo
> julia> f(x)
> 11
> ```
> 
> ---
> 
> If you show me the exact contents of `one.jl` and `two.jl`, I can suggest the cleanest arrangement (whether `x` should live in the module, be passed in, or be in a shared “config” module).
